Dasein wrote:
>
> OK I�ve decided to give rangers a go, for some strange reason.
> Anyway after reading the wiki, is it just me, or does is weapon
> skillset a big pile of crap?? For expert they can basically only get
> ranged weapons

The word "range" is a part of their class name.  They focus on missile
combat like wizards focus on spell combat - Not to exclusion but it is
their intended primary method.

> their Skilled weapons are of very questionable value
> (flail wtf?), and they can�t even get broadsword, let alone longsword,
> at basic. What am I supposed to melee with, a tin opener?

They get an initial dagger.  Name it and wield it until you have a
better option.  Sting counts as a better option.

As Zeitgeist posted gather daggers for throwing as soon as you can.

> Getting an
> artefact weapon unlocks that skill at basic, I believe, but still...
> I guess crysknife uses the knife skill, so that�s something. Or are
> rangers just never really supposed to melee at all?

It's my opinion they are supposed to use a gift artifact when they get
it, especially the crowning gift.  But until then use that initial
dagger to push your dagger skill towards expert asap.  Get Sting as soon
as you can then let any artifact gifts the game gives you determine your
backup weapon.  Playing a chaotic I like Stormbringer I tend to give up
wanting more artifacts when I get one that good or better.

It is no coincidence that there are many gnomes in the mines and gnomes
carry regular daggers. It is no coincidence that orcs appear early
on and orcs carry lesser daggers. It is no coincidence that elves
start appearing as you return from mines end and they carry the best
regularly available dagger. Get at least a dozen and then gradually
evolve your stack with better types until you have a dozen elven
daggers that stack. Then start enchanting them.  That's the same dagger
strategy I use for any class that can multifire daggers.  I do the same
with wizards and valkyries.

> Where am I
> supposed to get an infinite supply of arrows from?

Traps plus holy water plus arrow traps.  Use the mine town altar to push
your luck to maximum and all fountains in the game to dilute potions
until you have a large stack of blessed arrows and your luck is maxed
out.  From that point on you can start wielding your bow and using "x"
to switch to your backup wielded weapon when you are in trouble.  It is
no coincidence that you probably hit expert in dagger about the time you
return from mines end.  That works great for a cross aligned altar. 
Once your stack is blessed and your luck is at maximum the loss rate for
arrows is so low a size of a few dozen will last the rest of the game.

> The artefact
> Longbow I guess, which does sound very cool I must admit... free
> arrows and reflection...

But it's also a later game play I think.  It's your Quest artifact so
wishing for it is wastefull.  It also adds up that you likely hit expert
at bow by the time you get it.  You go from a dagger machine gun to an
arrow machine gun.  Always keeping the most powerful artifact gift
you've gotten so far as your alternate wield for when you encounter a
tight spot.

Once you have the Longbow after the Quest you will have a supply of
arrows so large you will be able to fire them and not even retrieve them
on plenty of levels.


